WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Hello!

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:03.290
Here are some bad news.

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:09.389
It seems like chatbots are the reason why
the future chatbots will not get smarter but

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:11.940
rather dumber than the current ones.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.310
Or at least, this is the worst-case scenario
this paper presents: if the future Internet

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:22.600
will be flooded by data generated by large
language models and the Internet will remain

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:28.410
the source of training data for future language
models, then these future models will degrade

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.590
a lot.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.730
In this video, we will discuss what the problems
are with training models on data from other

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:40.219
models, how likely it is that the internet
of the future will contain almost only language

00:00:40.219 --> 00:00:46.230
model generated data, which will help us to
assess if and how much this paper exaggerates

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.850
in its assumptions, which affects its conclusions.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:54.760
Do you ever wonder how the chatbots of the
future can become better than the existing

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:55.899
ones?

00:00:55.899 --> 00:01:01.399
By letting them ingest even more high-quality
data during training, or at least this seems

00:01:01.399 --> 00:01:07.270
like the way to do it without any other innovation
besides the engineering tricks we are already

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.530
using.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.540
And even with technical innovation, this is
machine learning after all, meaning that lots

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.470
of good training data is always a great idea.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:20.390
But there is a limit to how much training
data we can get in the future.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:25.189
Researchers extrapolated from past trends
how much unlabeled text and data

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:31.110
we can expect on the internet in the future
and conclude that high-quality text data will

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:37.610
be exhausted soon, likely before 2026 and
that low-quality text and image data will

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.500
be exhausted much later, between 2030 and
2050.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:46.719
But machine learning researchers do not act
like they are much concerned with the worry

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:52.640
that our data production and storage rate
might not keep up with the appetite of large

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:53.869
models for data.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:59.250
Who needs humans to produce data anyways,
because nowadays we can have generative AI

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.320
that can help with this!

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.320
Right?

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.009
Let’s see.

00:02:04.009 --> 00:02:09.080
Researchers lately did not shy away from training
large language models on data synthesized

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:10.670
by other models.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:16.150
The rationale of researchers at Stanford when
creating the Alpaca model was: We may not

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:23.640
have the data collection of OpenAI, but if
we finetune Meta AI’s LLaMA on data generated

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:31.340
by OpenAI’s text-davinci-003, then we can
make Alpaca mimic OpenAI’s model.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:37.280
The Koala model from UC Berkeley was trained
on data from ShareGPT, which is a Chrome extension

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.989
where users can share their conversations
with ChatGPT.

00:02:41.989 --> 00:02:46.650
Researchers have also used data generated
by models that were already trained with RLHF

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.209
to stand in for human demonstrations when
training other models with RLHF.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:56.810
But this paper says that there is a danger
when recursively training models,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:02.090
so it is a bad idea to train on data generated
by another model, which in turn was trained

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:05.459
on data from another model, and so on.

00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:10.769
It sounds an alarm signal that recursively
training a model on a previous model’s outputs,

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:15.780
can lead to worse and worse models with every
training generation, and can lead to very

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.400
stark degradation of the last generation models.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:23.799
At first, I thought the assumptions and experiment
settings of the paper are exaggerated and

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:28.450
we will discuss in the next part of the video
why they might be less exaggerated than I

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.909
initially thought.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:36.040
But first, let’s see the three illustrative
experiments from the paper about model degradation

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.870
when trained recursively.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:42.280
They are surprisingly simple, but bear with
them for a bit.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:48.680
First, the authors work with a simple 2-dimensional
dataset consisting of 1000 points belonging

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.010
to two Gaussian distributions.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:56.060
They fit a Gaussian Mixture Model onto the
data, which basically tries to find the parameters

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.770
of two Gaussians that best cluster the data.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.970
From the fitted model, they sample 1000 points
and fit another Gaussian Mixture model on

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:08.060
this, and this goes on and on for 2000 generations.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:14.159
And I know that 2000 generations seem a bit
over the top, but the point here is to see

00:04:14.159 --> 00:04:21.579
the bigger picture of what small degradations
in each generation lead to: at each generation,

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:27.490
the shape of the original data is degraded
and already at generation 100 becomes unrecognizable.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:34.440
At 2000 generations, they show that the estimation
of the distribution collapses into this tiny

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.100
region with very low spread.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.030
Why like this and not differently?

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:45.800
Because the models of the first generations
train on data sampled from the early generation.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:51.639
During sampling we see common events with
more probability and less common ones with

00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:56.900
less probability, so because later models
see rarer events less, they more and more

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.740
forget these rare events of low probabilities.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:05.430
The second reason is that later generations
of models entangle and remix different facts

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.310
that were previously learned well.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:13.130
The reason for this is that models either
underfit the data and entangle different facts,

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:25.220
or overfit outside the sampled distribution
and invent new data points.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:30.180
And this same message that we saw clearly
in the interpretable 2-dimensional space,

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:34.330
we see in the following more complicated experiments
as well.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:41.150
The authors train a Variational Autoencoder
on the MNIST dataset for 20 generations, where

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:46.660
each generation trains just on data sampled
from the previous generation, so no real data

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:48.100
whatsoever.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:51.190
We will discuss later whether this is exaggerated
or not.

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:56.970
The authors see that the Variational Autoencoder
at every generation degraded in its ability

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:03.840
to produce good looking digits and generation
20 produces all the same type of mixes of

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.050
digits.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:09.950
So as previously, the models converge to just
one type of data with low variance and forget

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:14.880
the specificities that make up each individual
digit.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.200
Then finally, language models.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.490
Unfortunately, the authors do not pretrain
any language model, because they say it is

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:23.490
costly.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:29.030
But arguably, they did not need to train GPT-3,
a small GPT-2 type model would have sufficed

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:30.320
to prove their point.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:38.130
Instead, they finetuned a pretrained OPT language
model of 125 million parameters on wikitext2

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:44.190
in two settings: In the first setting, each
generation could not see any original data,

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:47.130
but just data from the previous generation.

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:52.080
In the second setting, finally they let the
model see some real data at each generation,

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.430
namely 10% real data and 90% data sampled
from the previous generation.

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:04.139
And as before, but now with language, the
9th generation models assigned high probability

00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:10.699
to sequences that the original OPT model would
have never produced and started to talk gibberish,

00:07:10.699 --> 00:07:12.360
which OPT would have never done.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.340
The setting in which during training, each
model had access to 10% real data was generally

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.020
better than the setting without access to
real data.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:26.580
But now, is this even realistic to conduct
experiments with just 10% real data??

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:32.340
First when reading the paper, Ms. Coffee Bean
thought that the paper bluntly exaggerates

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.340
their estimations because, come on!

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:37.569
They do their analyses and their experiments
based on the assumption that models only have

00:07:37.569 --> 00:07:42.850
access to either no human produced data or
as little as 10%!

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.909
She thought this is a huge exaggeration and
I am just … confused why so much of the

00:07:47.909 --> 00:07:55.050
Internet would be produced by AI -- in my
naivete: Only because we will use AI writing

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.449
assistants?

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:02.069
When writing, the interesting stuff is new
information, where language models can only

00:08:02.069 --> 00:08:07.350
help a bit with the formulation, but not with
coming up with the new idea.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:11.970
Yann LeCun was pointing out that the problem
with AI generated content is the dissemination,

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:12.979
not the writing.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:17.669
The people using AI generated content would
have so much trouble finding people to read

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:21.229
the AI generated content, that they would
leave it be.

00:08:21.229 --> 00:08:26.440
But I must admit I had to change my mind a
bit and now I try to convince Ms. Coffee Bean

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:27.680
as well.

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:32.599
There are multiple events that are not just
speculations, but are happening right now,

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:37.620
maybe you have heard of some of them yourself:
For one: When googling whether there is a

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:42.729
country in Africa that starts with k, this
thing comes up, which is clearly produced

00:08:42.729 --> 00:08:44.209
by a language model.

00:08:44.209 --> 00:08:48.340
It seems like there is at least enough AI
generated content such that search engines

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.910
draw that garbage in and highlight it.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:56.459
Then, we see that there are ways of using
AIs to produce content farms sites which have

00:08:56.459 --> 00:09:01.670
successfully squeezed money from at least
140 major brands.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:02.860
How is this possible?

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:08.240
Well, because ads are often placed programmatically,
and until ad placement companies such as Google

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.610
Ads do not do something about this, the problem
will not stop.

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:18.399
These so-called news websites produce hundreds
of articles per day, for example by scraping

00:09:18.399 --> 00:09:23.279
reddit and letting AI write something based
on the results.

00:09:23.279 --> 00:09:28.970
In one case, they finally got caught when
people on the World of Warcraft subreddit

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:34.500
convinced the bot to publish news about the
release of a character that was entirely made

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:40.250
up and even included the sentence: “I just
really want some major bot operated news websites

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:42.519
to publish an article about this”.

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:45.880
But who knows how many more sites operate
like this?

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:52.180
To underline this, a new study from Europol
estimates that 90% of the Internet will be

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.519
AI generated by 2026.

00:09:55.519 --> 00:09:57.050
What do you think about it?

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:01.420
Because if we are to believe this, then the
authors were not at all too pessimistic when

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:08.029
conducting their experiments on 90% model
generated content and just 10% real data!

00:10:08.029 --> 00:10:10.000
So what to do in this whole situation?

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.810
On the one hand, we have this paper making
experiments in this worst-case scenario setting

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:21.839
of having little to no human generated data,
but did not experiment with pretraining large

00:10:21.839 --> 00:10:25.899
models like ChatGPT, but only with finetuning.

00:10:25.899 --> 00:10:30.839
On the other hand, we have papers that have
no troubles training on model generated data,

00:10:30.839 --> 00:10:35.560
especially to mimic with smaller models the
capabilities of larger models.

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:39.750
But these procedures are to be taken with
care, especially in the large language model

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.670
field where evaluation is extremely hard.

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:48.490
It could be (and some people also think that)
these models trained on synthetic data show

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:53.740
great benchmarking scores and are as good
as ChatGPT on the benchmarks because they

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:59.720
mimicked its outputs, but they lack in everything
else and do not cover the huge application

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:05.829
diversity of the models have that were trained
with actual human data.

00:11:05.829 --> 00:11:12.360
Ms. Coffee Bean is still undecided whether
recursive training is that bad or really unfixable.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:18.370
I think, that until we know it, we have one
more reason to tag and watermark AI generated

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:19.370
content.

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:24.779
We should do it just for the record, so when
training the next generation of AI models,

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:30.709
we can choose to easily filter out AI generated
content.

00:11:30.709 --> 00:11:34.990
Are you worried about AI generated content
flooding the internet?

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:39.300
We’ll read what you think in the comments
and hope to see you next time!

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:39.399
Okay, bye!

